<TopicPage slug="subjects/history/intermediate/the-treaty-of-versailles">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This intermediate History lesson builds on the foundational <em>The World Wars</em>:
    you need the war's shape — armistice, costs, collapse.
    Sibling <em>The League of Nations</em> continues the story — the League was
    Versailles's own institution. Every section feeds the essay question "was the treaty
    fair?"
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>state what Wilson, Clemenceau and Lloyd George each wanted, and to whom each answered;</li>
    <li>explain why none of the victors got everything they wanted;</li>
    <li>set out the treaty's terms — war guilt, reparations, disarmament, territory — and what each was designed to do;</li>
    <li>trace the consequences in Germany to 1923, including the Ruhr and hyperinflation;</li>
    <li>argue both sides of the fairness question with contemporary evidence.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    In January 1919 the victors of the greatest war in history gathered in Paris to rebuild
    the world, and produced the document this page dissects. The exam question it exists for
    is blunt: <strong>Was the Treaty of Versailles fair?</strong> Answer it in the syllabus's
    four moves — what did Wilson, Clemenceau and Lloyd George each want; why did none of them
    get everything; what did the treaty do to Germany by the end of 1923; and could it be
    justified at the time? Keep one thought in view throughout: <strong>the treaty's greatest
    cost was not measured in gold marks but in legitimacy</strong>. The Germans who had to
    live under it were never allowed to agree with it, and a peace the defeated hate is a
    debt the future pays.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="bet before you read on — the real bill" widget="predict" config={toeLegitPredict} />

  <Widget no="1" caption="the Big Three: three mandates, one compromise" widget="equationStep" config={bigThreeCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the five cards pair each mandate with its compromise.
    Watch the incompatibility: Wilson's self-determination applied only to the enemies'
    empires, Clemenceau's security had no guaranteed signatories, Lloyd George's
    tightrope produced a treaty harsh enough to wound, not to cripple. Card 5 previews the
    fairness argument.
  </p>

  <h2>The motives and aims of the Big Three</h2>
  <p>
    First, the setting, because it explains the mood. The Paris Peace Conference opened on
    18 January 1919 — deliberately the anniversary of the German Empire's proclamation at
    Versailles in 1871 — and it was run by a "Council of Four" (Wilson, Clemenceau, Lloyd
    George, and Italy's Orlando, who mattered least): some thirty nations attended, but the
    decisions were made in small rooms by five men. Germany was not invited to negotiate, and
    defeated Russia, mid-civil-war, was not invited at all. Six months of conference produced
    a settlement drafted under enormous pressure: empires were collapsing across Europe,
    revolution was in the air, and the delegates' mailbags carried a million names on
    petitions from every borderland in Europe.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="pair each setting detail with its meaning" widget="match" config={toeSettingMatch} />
  <p>
    Woodrow Wilson arrived believing he was writing the constitution of a new world order.
    His Fourteen Points (January 1918) — open diplomacy, freedom of the seas, self-determination,
    a League of Nations — had helped persuade Germany to armistice in the first place, and
    Wilson expected them to be the peace's foundation. Georges Clemenceau, Prime Minister of
    France, had different arithmetic in his head: France had been invaded twice in his
    lifetime, had lost about 1.3 million dead and had its richest regions wrecked. "Le Tigre"
    wanted security made permanent — Germany disarmed, made to pay, and if possible pinned
    behind a French-friendly Rhineland. David Lloyd George, Britain's Prime Minister, was the
    tightrope-walker: newly re-elected on rhetoric promising to squeeze "the German lemon till
    the pips squeak", he privately wanted a middle peace — punishment enough for British
    public opinion, but a Germany whole enough to buy British goods and, crucially, to stand
    as a buffer against Bolshevism, which everyone at Paris feared was spreading west out of
    Russia. Each man also answered to a different public: Wilson to a Congress that would
    ultimately vote his peace down, Clemenceau to a parliament that nearly toppled him for
    being too soft, Lloyd George to an electorate drunk on victory.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="find the faulty line — the three mandates" widget="spot-error" config={toeAimsSpot} />
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> the Big Three were free agents over an empty map. Each
    answered to a public with its own arithmetic, so the compromise was
    constrained before the first session began — judging the treaty means judging 1919's
    politics, not just its clauses.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> three incompatible mandates met in Paris; the treaty is their
    residue. Next: what it actually said, clause by clause.
  </p>

  <h2>Why the victors did not get everything they wanted</h2>
  <p>
    The treaty is best read as the residue of three incompatible victories. Wilson got his
    League — written into every treaty — but lost self-determination everywhere it was
    inconvenient: it applied to the enemies' empires in Europe but not to the victors' colonies
    (which became "mandates" — empires by another name), not to the three million
    German-speakers handed to Czechoslovakia and Poland, and not to Austria, which wanted
    union with Germany and was forbidden it by Article 80. Worst of all, Wilson could not
    carry his own country: the Senate rejected the treaty in November 1919 and again in March
    1920, and the United States signed a separate peace — leaving the League, from birth,
    without its architect. Clemenceau kept Alsace-Lorraine, reparations, occupation rights in
    the Rhineland and an Anglo-American guarantee of French security — but the guarantee was
    never ratified (it died with the treaty in Washington), and the Rhineland stayed German;
    he was denounced at home as having been deceived. Lloyd George got most of the German
    fleet, the German colonies as mandates, and a Germany trimmed on its borders — but the
    naval hail of German ships sank at Scapa Flow, and by 1922 he was privately calling the
    treaty a mistake. Even the lesser victors felt cheated: Italy left Paris calling its
    reward a "mutilated victory" (Fiume went unclaimed), Japan won Shandong — which so
    angered China that China refused to sign at all. When everyone feels robbed, the
    settlement has no constituency for defending it.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="drag the divider — promised, then delivered" widget="compare" config={toeResidueCompare} />

  <h2>What the treaty actually said</h2>
  <p>
    Everything rested on <strong>Article 231</strong>, the "war guilt clause", which declared
    Germany and its allies responsible "for all the loss and damage" of the war. This was not
    primarily a moral verdict — it was a legal hinge: reparations could only be billed to a
    power legally responsible for the damage. On that hinge hung the bill (fixed by the London
    Schedule of May 1921 at 132 billion gold marks, about £6.6 billion), and the disarmament
    clauses: an army of 100,000 long-service volunteers, no conscription, no tanks, no air
    force, no submarines, a navy capped at six battleships, and the Rhineland demilitarised
    so France would always have a head start if Germany moved. Territory went in every
    direction: Alsace-Lorraine to France; Eupen-Malmedy to Belgium; North Schleswig to Denmark
    after a plebiscite; West Prussia and Posen to the new Poland, cutting East Prussia off
    behind the Polish Corridor, with Danzig a "free city"; the Saar coalfields run by the
    League for fifteen years; and every colony converted into a League mandate administered
    by a victor. In all, Germany lost about 13 per cent of its territory, a tenth of its
    population, and nearly half its iron-ore reserves — plus, by separate settlements,
    most of its merchant fleet and foreign investments. Allied troops would occupy the
    Rhineland's bridgeheads for fifteen years; some territories were handed over after
    plebiscites (Schleswig, Upper Silesia), which made those borders harder for Germans to
    hate — an honesty the rest of the treaty lacked. The terms were harsh enough to wound
    and not harsh enough to cripple: Germany remained the largest nation in Europe west of
    Russia, and every German knew it.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="call the bill — the London Schedule" widget="estimate" config={toeTermsEstimate} />
  <Widget no="8" caption="place the share — the map shrinks" widget="place" config={toeTermsPlace} />

    Russia, and every German knew it.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> the war guilt clause was a moral accusation — Germans
    branded as the war's only sinners. As drafted, Article 231 was a legal hinge:
    reparations could only be billed to a power legally responsible for the damage, so the
    clause existed to make the bill enforceable. The tragedy was the presentation — a legal
    device read as an insult.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="from armistice to Ruhr: how the treaty landed on Germany, 1918–24" widget="timeline" config={timelineCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the timeline runs from armistice to Dawes Plan — six
    years of the treaty's words becoming lived experience. Notice the causal chain:
    missed coal deliveries, the Ruhr occupation, passive resistance paid for by printing,
    the mark at 4.2 trillion to the dollar. Each event feeds the next — the chain a
    consequences question asks you to reconstruct.
  </p>

  <h2>Germany under the treaty, 1919–23</h2>
  <p>
    Germans called it a <strong>Diktat</strong>: no German representative was allowed to
    negotiate — only to receive, comment and sign — and the delegates signed on 28 June 1919
    only under threat of renewed invasion. Because the armistice had come before any enemy
    soldier crossed Germany's borders, the army's myth of the <em>Dolchstoß</em> — the "stab
    in the back" supposedly delivered by civilians and Socialists at home — took root
    instantly, and the politicians who signed the peace became "the November criminals". The
    treaty thus poisoned the new Republic at birth: every future crisis could be hung around
    Versailles's neck. Political violence became routine — in 1921 the foreign minister
    Walther Rathenau, a Jew who had organised the war economy, was machine-gunned in the
    street by right-wing extremists, one of hundreds of assassinations of the period. The
    first coup attempt came quickly — the Kapp Putsch of March 1920, when
    demobilised right-wing veterans seized Berlin and were defeated only by a general strike.
    Then came the crisis of 1923, the treaty's economics turned physical: when Germany
    defaulted on the coal deliveries owed in kind, French and Belgian troops occupied the
    Ruhr. Berlin ordered passive resistance; the Ruhr stopped producing; and the government
    paid the idled workforce by printing money. By November 1923 one US dollar bought 4.2
    trillion marks, savings and pensions evaporated, and with them the middle classes'
    loyalty to the Republic. A new currency (the Rentenmark) and the Dawes Plan of 1924 —
    American loans cycling through Germany to France and Britain and back to America —
    restored stability, but on a foundation of US credit that would collapse with Wall Street
    in 1929.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="line up the crisis chain, earliest first" widget="order" config={toeCrisisOrder} />
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> hyperinflation was "caused by reparations". The chain was
    longer: reparations were the trigger, but the Ruhr occupation, the deliberate policy of
    paying for passive resistance with newly printed money (rather than taxing anyone), and a
    pre-existing debt-financed war economy all had to come together to kill the mark. Germany
    had printed money throughout the war; prices quadrupled, not quadrillion-fold. The
    lesson: hyperinflation was a policy failure compounded by the treaty, not a reparations
    invoice come due.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the terms are on the table and Germany's 1919-23 written
    under them — a poisoned Republic, a dead mark, stabilisation on American credit. Next:
    could it have been justified with 1919's eyes rather than hindsight's?
  </p>

  <h2>Could the Treaty be justified at the time?</h2>
  <p>
    The syllabus asks for <em>contemporary</em> opinions, so build the case from 1919, not
    hindsight. On the prosecuting side: the treaty was markedly milder than the one Germany
    had itself imposed on Russia at Brest-Litovsk in March 1918 — taking about a third of
    Russia's population and most of its industry — so Germany's outrage looked selective;
    northern France really did lie in ruins, its mines flooded and its towns razed; and a
    power that had invaded Belgium in 1914 and shot civilians en route had little standing to
    complain of severity. On the defending side: John Maynard Keynes, who quit the British
    delegation, published <em>The Economic Consequences of the Peace</em> within six months,
    warning that a "Carthaginian peace" would bankrupt Europe together — and he was broadly
    right about the 1920s, if he exaggerated what came after; Marshal Foch called the treaty
    "an armistice for twenty years" and was exact to the season; and many French thought the
    treaty <em>too soft</em>, since Germany remained intact and potentially stronger than
    before. The deepest judgement distinguishes content from form: the terms were arguably
    defensible for a power that had started (or, at minimum, lost) the war, but the process —
    a dictated signature, a war-guilt clause that read as an insult, promises made in Wilson's
    Points that Paris quietly broke — ensured the terms were never accepted as legitimate
    even where they could be justified. Versailles failed less as a bill than as a settlement.
  </p>
  <Widget no="10" caption="judge it with 1919's eyes" widget="branch" config={toe1919Branch} />

  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> Versailles was uniquely harsh. Germany's own record says
    otherwise: at Brest-Litovsk in March 1918 it imposed on Russia a peace taking a third
    of its population and most of its industry — far harsher than Versailles. That does not
    settle fairness, but disarms the claim that Germany was treated as no power had ever
    been.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>What did each of the Big Three want from the peace — and what did each actually get?</li>
    <li>Why did Germany call the treaty a Diktat, and how did that feed the stab-in-the-back myth?</li>
    <li>Was the treaty defensible at the time? Give one argument each way.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Wilson wanted peace without victory and a League — he
    got the League, lost self-determination where inconvenient, and his Senate rejected
    it. Clemenceau wanted security — he kept Alsace-Lorraine, reparations and a
    demilitarised Rhineland, but the guarantee died unratified. Lloyd George
    wanted a middle peace — the colonies and a trimmed Germany, by 1922 privately
    calling the treaty a mistake. (2) No German was allowed to negotiate — only to receive,
    comment and sign under threat of invasion — and because the armistice came before any
    enemy soldier crossed the border, the stab-in-the-back myth claimed the war was lost at
    home; the signers became the "November criminals". (3) For: milder than Brest-Litovsk,
    northern France in ruins, and the disarmament terms defensible against a power that had
    started (or at minimum lost) the war. Against: Keynes predicted a Carthaginian peace,
    Foch an armistice for twenty years, and the dictated process meant the terms were
    never accepted as legitimate.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The treaty was the residue of three incompatible victories — none of the Big Three got what they wanted.</li>
    <li>Article 231 was a legal hinge for reparations but read as a moral insult — legitimacy, not arithmetic, was the treaty's fatal currency.</li>
    <li>Terms: 132 billion gold marks, an army of 100,000, territory in every direction, colonies as mandates — 13 per cent of land, a tenth of the people.</li>
    <li>Germans experienced the peace as a Diktat signed under threat — it poisoned the Republic at birth.</li>
    <li>Hyperinflation was a chain — Ruhr occupation, passive resistance, printing — reparations the trigger, not the sole cause.</li>
    <li>Verdicts split: milder than Brest-Litovsk, or Keynes's Carthaginian peace and Foch's twenty-year armistice.</li>
    <li>A peace the defeated hate is a debt the future pays — the greatest cost was legitimacy.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    <em>The League of Nations</em> is the direct sequel — the institution Versailles created
    to hold it together; <em>Colonialism and Empire</em> follows the mandate
    system by which colonies changed hands; and the grievances documented here return
    in <em>The World Wars</em> as foundations of the Second.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Three expert refinements earn their keep. First, the war-guilt question has its own
    history: in the 1960s the German historian Fritz Fischer reopened Article 231 with
    archival evidence that Germany's leadership had deliberately launched a war of expansion
    — a finding that, ironically, made the treaty's most hated clause more defensible than
    its own drafters could prove in 1919. Second, the reparations were never actually paid as
    billed: Germany paid roughly an eighth of the 132 billion marks before the whole system
    was wound up at Lausanne in 1932 — the bill's political symbolism mattered far more than
    its arithmetic. Third, Versailles was never one treaty but a family of them — Saint-Germain
    with Austria, Trianon with Hungary (which lost two-thirds of its population), Neuilly,
    Sèvres (repudiated by Turkish nationalists, replaced at Lausanne) — and the Paris
    settlement's real weakness was that it redrew the map of Central Europe around
    nation-states without the economic, military or American power to maintain it. The
    treaty's own architects half-knew this: asked how the settlement would look in history,
    Clemenceau is said to have replied, "The future? It is a very long time." The next lesson
    follows the institution Versailles created to hold it together: the League of Nations.
  </p>

